PART 2 — THE RE-ANCHORED PYTEST GATE 4
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
Harness: a COPY of supabase/migrations/ under a scratch tree, with the gate
module's `_migrations_dir()` monkeypatched at it. ⛔ The tracked repo is not
written at ANY point in this part, so no restore of a repo file is involved and
no sha256 pair is recorded for it — the pristine COPY is re-verified GREEN after
every neuter instead, which is the equivalent proof for a harness that owns its
own tree.

Command, from analytics-service/:
    python3 neuter_gate4.py     (see the plan's SUMMARY for the scratch path)

BASELINE: gate 4 GREEN on the pristine copy of the migration tree
N-P1  RED    IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE -> <> TRUE (fanout arm)
       LEDGER-02 LOCK B: the fan-out body does not compare the activation value with `IF v_enabled IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE THEN`. That exact form is the fail-closed truth table: it is TRUE for FALSE and TRUE for NULL, and NULL is what BOTH a missing row and a failed read produce. Any NULL-unsafe form falls through on those two paths and opens the flag precisely when the switch could not be read.
N-P2  RED    handler assigns TRUE instead of NULL (fanout arm)
       LEDGER-02 LOCK B: the fan-out body's activation-read handler assigns `v_enabled := NULL;` 0 time(s), expected exactly 1. A handler that leaves the variable untouched, or assigns TRUE, is a fail-OPEN switch wearing a fail-closed shape: the read raised and the fan-out proceeds anyway.
N-P3  RED    a statement inserted BEFORE the guard's inner BEGIN (fanout arm)
       LEDGER-02 LOCK B: the activation guard is NOT the first statement of the fan-out body. 161.1's own comment calls that placement deliberate, and the reason is concrete: a guard that runs after the advisory lock, the candidate query or the enqueue loop is a guard that runs after work has already happened. 'Present somewhere in the body' is not the property this switch needs.
N-P4  RED    the read DELETED (0 reads) — the switch is gone (fanout arm)
       LEDGER-02 LOCK B: the fan-out body does not read the activation flag from public.system_flags. Lock B is what makes merging the migration behaviour-neutral, and it is the incident-pressure kill switch (rollback level 1 in the runbook). Without it this body fans out unconditionally on every tick.
N-P5  RED    IF NOT v_enabled THEN — the NULL-unsafe truthiness form (fanout arm)
       LEDGER-02 LOCK B: the fan-out body does not compare the activation value with `IF v_enabled IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE THEN`. That exact form is the fail-closed truth table: it is TRUE for FALSE and TRUE for NULL, and NULL is what BOTH a missing row and a failed read produce. Any NULL-unsafe form falls through on those two paths and opens the flag precisely when the switch could not be read.
N-P6  RED    NULL-unsafe spelling ADDED BESIDE a correct guard — proves assertion (6) can be the FIRST failure rather than riding on (5)
       LEDGER-02 LOCK B: the fan-out body contains ['IF NOT v_enabled THEN']. With v_enabled NULL — the state a MISSING ROW and a FAILED READ both produce — each of those evaluates to NULL, the IF is not taken, and the body falls THROUGH to the fan-out. They open the flag on exactly the failure path this guard exists for. This is the boolean form of the F16 defect: a comparison that reads as correct and is not.
RESTORED: pristine copy GREEN after every neuter (6 of them)

